# `cp .env.example .env`, then fill in every `change-me`. In production these
# come from SSM and are scoped per service by compose.yaml; nothing hands one
# file to every container.
#
# THE SHIPPED POSTURE IS A HOSTNAME DEPLOYMENT OVER HTTPS. Substitute your own
# name for routes.example.org in the four places it appears below - the Caddy
# site address, ALLOWED_HOSTS, CSRF_TRUSTED_ORIGINS and the Discord redirect -
# and the four agree by construction. A local plain-HTTP stack is the commented
# block further down, and it is five lines because it is five lines: the four
# above with local values, plus DJANGO_DEBUG=1, which has no counterpart up
# here and is the one whose omission makes a `:80` stack impossible to sign in
# to. The example that shipped before this one mixed the two postures and
# nobody could sign in on it; the one after that counted this block as four,
# leaving DJANGO_DEBUG out, which is the same defect a step removed.
#
# Why the two cannot be mixed: `settings.py` sets
# `SESSION_COOKIE_SECURE = not DEBUG` and `CSRF_COOKIE_SECURE = not DEBUG`, so
# with DJANGO_DEBUG unset every cookie the site issues is marked Secure and a
# browser talking plain HTTP to a `:80` stack throws both of them away. The
# login round-trip completes, Discord redirects back, and the next request
# arrives with no session - a sign-in that fails with no error anywhere. A
# `:80` stack therefore needs DJANGO_DEBUG=1 as well, which is why the local
# block sets it and why it is a local block.

# --- Filling in the `change-me` values ---------------------------------------
#
# DJANGO_SECRET_KEY, KEY_ENCRYPTION_KEY, PGPASSWORD and BOT_INTERNAL_SECRET are
# each a fresh random string per deployment, and there is no reason to invent
# one by hand:
#
#     python -c "import secrets; print(secrets.token_urlsafe(48))"
#
# Use the generator. It emits URL-safe base64 - letters, digits, `-` and `_` -
# and every rule below is about characters it cannot produce.
#
# Two things about the values that are not obvious from looking at this file:
#
#   - `$` is compose's own interpolation character, and this file is parsed by
#     compose, not by a shell. Measured against the Docker CLI this repository
#     renders with, one line at a time, `PGPASSWORD=...` and the value the
#     container receives:
#
#         pa$w0rd        ->  pa         ($w0rd is a name; it expands to nothing
#                                        and compose only warns)
#         pa${w0}rd      ->  pard       (same, braced)
#         'pa$w0rd'      ->  pa$w0rd    <- write it like this
#         "pa$w0rd"      ->  pa         (double quotes do NOT protect it)
#         pa$$w0rd       ->  pa$w0rd    (the `$$` escape; correct but per-`$`)
#         'pa$$w0rd'     ->  pa$$w0rd   (inside single quotes `$$` is two `$`)
#
#     So: **if a value you paste from elsewhere contains a `$`, wrap the whole
#     value in single quotes.** Compose's parser strips them and takes
#     everything between them literally - no expansion, no escapes, nothing to
#     count - which is one rule instead of one decision per character. The
#     `$$` form works too and is what this file used to prescribe; it is worse
#     only in that it has to be applied to each `$` and is wrong for a value
#     that genuinely contains two in a row. A value that itself contains a
#     single quote cannot be single-quoted; pick another password.
#
#     This is also why nothing in this file may be `set -a; . ./.env; set +a`'d
#     into a shell before running `docker compose`. The shell has a third set
#     of rules - `$$` is the shell's own pid, backticks run commands - and an
#     exported value wins over the file, so the value compose gets is whatever
#     the shell made of it. docs/DEPLOYMENT.md sources this file nowhere.
#
#   - PGPASSWORD must not be empty. The postgis image refuses to initialise
#     without POSTGRES_PASSWORD - "Database is uninitialized and superuser
#     password is not specified" - and it fails at initdb, so the failure is a
#     database that never comes up rather than one that is open.
#
# Quotes are the parser's and not part of the value: compose's dotenv reader
# strips a matching pair of either kind. That is the opposite of what this file
# used to claim ("quotes around a value become part of the value"), and it is
# what makes the single-quoted form above work. Nothing else here needs them,
# and nothing else here has them.

TAG=dev

# The data volume. Everything durable binds under this, so nothing with state in
# it lands on the root volume alongside the OS, the images and the code.
DATA_ROOT=/srv/routemaker/data

# --- The public origin. These four are one decision, not four. ---------------
#
# The edge, and the caddy service alone. The address Caddy serves on, read by
# the Caddyfile through Caddy's own `{$VAR}` substitution: a hostname gets an
# automatic certificate from Let's Encrypt, which commits this host to serving
# that name - it has to resolve here and ports 80 and 443 have to be reachable
# from outside before the stack comes up.
CADDY_SITE_ADDRESS=routes.example.org

DJANGO_SECRET_KEY=change-me
# Every host name this deployment answers to. Caddy passes the browser's Host
# through, so a name missing here is a DisallowedHost 400 on every request.
DJANGO_ALLOWED_HOSTS=routes.example.org
# Scheme *and* host, and the scheme is what the browser used - which is https,
# because Caddy redirects http to https for a named site. An `https://` origin
# in front of a `:80` stack is the mismatch that shipped here before: every
# POST from the site itself was refused as a CSRF origin failure.
DJANGO_CSRF_TRUSTED_ORIGINS=https://routes.example.org

# Where the admin - and with it the operations page at
# `<path>core/scheduledrun/` - is mounted. The default below is the one in
# `settings.py`, it is in this repository, and it is therefore public: change it
# on any deployment reachable from the internet. It is obscurity and not a
# control - the disabled login form, the derived staff resolution and the
# per-object checks are what actually protect the admin - but an unadvertised
# path keeps the login form out of everybody's scanner logs. Must end in a `/`.
#
# DJANGO_ADMIN_PATH=internal-8f3a/

# The database host. Commented out on purpose, and this is not a stylistic
# choice: compose.yaml reads `${PGHOST:-postgis}`, so a value here *overrides*
# that default for all four Django services, and the value that used to be
# here - 127.0.0.1 - is the api, worker, migrate or rebuild container itself,
# where nothing listens on 5432. Rendered, `migrate` failed to connect, and
# `api`, `worker` and `rebuild` all wait on migrate completing, so the whole
# stack came up as Caddy and three routers. Leave it commented and the service
# name is used. Set it only to point at a database outside this stack.
#
# PGHOST=postgis
PGDATABASE=routemaker
PGUSER=routemaker
# Also the postgis image's POSTGRES_PASSWORD, and that is the whole of the
# catch: the image reads POSTGRES_PASSWORD only when it initialises an empty
# PGDATA. Once the volume exists the role keeps the password it was created
# with, changing this line alone changes nothing in the database, and the
# health check does not notice - pg_isready reports PQPING_OK for a server that
# would reject the connection on the password, so the gate goes green, migrate
# fails authentication, and api, worker and rebuild never start. To rotate it:
# ALTER ROLE inside the running postgis container first, then this line, then
# `docker compose up -d`. docs/DEPLOYMENT.md, "Secrets, and what rotating one
# costs", has the commands.
PGPASSWORD=change-me

# Every service that imports Django settings. The ban tombstone's HMAC key is
# derived from it with a domain-separated label, so the encryption key and the
# tombstone key are two distinct values from one delivered secret. There is no
# default: a process without this fails at import rather than keying every
# tombstone in the deployment with a value that is in the repository.
KEY_ENCRYPTION_KEY=change-me

# Discord sign-in, API only. All three reach the `api` service and nothing else.
#
# The client id and the redirect URI are not secrets - both appear in every
# authorize URL the browser follows - but they are what makes the login path
# work: without them the URL names an empty application and sends the browser
# back to localhost after Discord approves it. The redirect must match one of
# the redirect URIs registered on the Discord application exactly, character
# for character, and it must be a URL this stack actually serves: the same
# scheme, the same host as DJANGO_ALLOWED_HOSTS, and a port Caddy publishes
# (80 and 443, and no others - it is the only service with a `ports:` entry).
# The value that shipped before this one named port 8000, which nothing in the
# stack publishes at all.
DISCORD_CLIENT_ID=change-me
DISCORD_REDIRECT_URI=https://routes.example.org/auth/callback
DISCORD_CLIENT_SECRET=change-me

# The one bootstrap Discord id, and the only way a new deployment gets its first
# instance admin - there is no password login and no createsuperuser here. It
# grants standing only while the instance-admin list is empty; the first admin
# request under it writes the id into the list, audits that, and disables this
# path for good. Leave it unset once the deployment has an instance admin.
BOOTSTRAP_INSTANCE_ADMIN_DISCORD_ID=

# How long the removal of an instance admin other than yourself waits before it
# takes effect, during which any instance admin can cancel it. Defaults to an
# hour; self-removal is immediate.
INSTANCE_ADMIN_REMOVAL_DELAY_SECONDS=3600

# The bot service only. It is the one component that holds this.
DISCORD_BOT_TOKEN=change-me
BOT_INTERNAL_SECRET=change-me

# gunicorn's worker count on the api service. Commented out because
# compose.yaml carries the default that matches that service's cpu limit -
# `${WEB_CONCURRENCY:-5}`, which is `2 * 2 + 1` against its two cpus. Set it
# here only together with the `cpus:` limit on that service; they are one
# decision, and each worker is a full Django process inside the same 2 GB.
# Left to the image to work out, the entrypoint reads the container's cgroup
# cpu quota - not `nproc`, which reports the host and on an 8-vCPU box asked
# for 17 workers inside that limit.
#
# WEB_CONCURRENCY=5

# The rebuild's source extract (docs/OPERATIONS.md, "The source extract"). All
# optional: leave them empty for the defaults. A comma-separated list of mirror
# URLs for the three Geofabrik files; the age in days past which the extract is
# downloaded again (default 6, just under the weekly cadence); set the third to
# 1 to force a refresh on the next rebuild; a polygon file to clip to instead
# of the bounding box in settings. A polygon must keep all of PLAN:13's region,
# western Loudoun and the north to the Mason-Dixon line included.
SOURCE_EXTRACT_URLS=
SOURCE_EXTRACT_MAX_AGE_DAYS=6
SOURCE_EXTRACT_FORCE_REFRESH=
COVERAGE_POLYGON=

# The tile template the admin's jurisdiction map draws under the polygon. API
# only, optional, and commented out because there is nothing correct to set it
# to yet: the self-hosted basemap (PLAN:15 - a PMTiles extract served by Caddy)
# is unbuilt in phase 1, and the public OpenStreetMap tile servers are the thing
# that line rules out. Left unset the map still draws, still edits and still
# saves - it simply has a plain background and asks nobody for tiles, which is
# what an instance admin editing a boundary actually needs.
#
# Set it only to a tile server you are entitled to use: this deployment's own
# renderer once there is one, or a server an operator has chosen. Standard XYZ
# placeholders, left alone by everything between here and OpenLayers.
#
# Note that compose.yaml does not pass this through to the api service yet
# (tests/test_compose.py records that), so setting it here has no effect until
# that line exists.
#
# ADMIN_BASEMAP_TILE_URL=https://tiles.example.org/basemap/{z}/{x}/{y}.png

# --- Local plain-HTTP stack: five lines, four of them in place of the four above
#
# For a stack on a laptop with no name and no certificate. DJANGO_DEBUG is not
# optional here and it is not a convenience: without it the session and CSRF
# cookies are Secure (settings.py derives both from DEBUG) and a plain-HTTP
# browser silently discards them, so sign-in appears to succeed and every later
# request is anonymous. Caddy publishes 80, so the redirect URI has no port -
# and http://localhost/auth/callback has to be registered on the Discord
# application as well, alongside the deployment's own.
#
# Never on a host reachable from outside: DEBUG serves tracebacks with settings
# in them, and cookies that are not Secure travel in clear.
#
# CADDY_SITE_ADDRESS=:80
# DJANGO_DEBUG=1
# DJANGO_ALLOWED_HOSTS=localhost,127.0.0.1
# DJANGO_CSRF_TRUSTED_ORIGINS=http://localhost,http://127.0.0.1
# DISCORD_REDIRECT_URI=http://localhost/auth/callback
